[{"id":"VRCE-1-23-1","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":23,"verse":1,"english":"Then next morning which happened to be fine, those repressors of their foes, with Viśvāmitra at their head, came to the banks of the river.* Ganga. The legend is that when the saint Agastya had sucked up the ocean, the Ganges replenished it.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.873,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-24-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1023001","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"adf0ee5f53b8c00ae2288bdcea5a6f3bf8d327bbd488e0d6d3180f2628c40392","compact":"5c74b5b4f29527821857f0b7ecce8695111e0397ab0ccdf5b4839e9fb6a70773","nasal":"5c74b5b4f29527821857f0b7ecce8695111e0397ab0ccdf5b4839e9fb6a70773"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-23-2","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":23,"verse":2,"english":"And those high-souled ascetics observing vows, having brought an elegant bark, addressed Viśvāmitra, saying.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-24-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1023002","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6401527015bbe15d4dcd2e1155dee804763690fa9a642915dc2d2b6807583b2b","compact":"e6336c0addf0219c108fe86d493b22d7b9a7e9a2b33bae949d33420e1375dd1c","nasal":"e6336c0addf0219c108fe86d493b22d7b9a7e9a2b33bae949d33420e1375dd1c"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-23-3","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":23,"verse":3,"english":"Do you ascend the bark, with the princes at your head! May your journey be auspicious: let no delay occur","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-24-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1023003","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"52c6c1efb7b4a51ab86e174316323d83cc53a508d2347e3531ba65c15d13292b","compact":"f910925e516d7ff774e87181d155fed827e1cfea160e8838053495a8ebf22f26","nasal":"6ff2ca109cf5b93a85e5f2bc616f477bf95de49053fc43d1ad17e5b1454abaa6"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-23-4","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":23,"verse":4,"english":"Thereupon saying, So be it! and having paid homage to those ascetics, Viśvāmitra set about crossing that river, which had replenished the ocean.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.788,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-24-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1023004","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"8657f1ffb0d01f024aacd6a9a21a81ea38ec276e289a65ec79565d29742ad99d","compact":"0c371e1660a576c606fefbc576579dc688cffb09d385f8cc32b6af3742cf41fb","nasal":"0c371e1660a576c606fefbc576579dc688cffb09d385f8cc32b6af3742cf41fb"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-23-5","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":23,"verse":5,"english":"And reaching the middle of the river, Rāma asked that best of ascetics, What is this loud uproar that seem to come riving the waters?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.871,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-24-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1023005","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6247efb8d5286720acf290fed10de4f5e0c62944c040a681282d0410522fab55","compact":"858a215e5ef5d29181150a6f9b40a9a5c258536450e9bbaaae10dd02ae9ff3ce","nasal":"6e50d366edd8c7a3cc29d07888743e61e4c71e54a8da280cfb0f8df4a89a44e3"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-23-6","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":23,"verse":6,"english":"Hearing Rāghava's words dictated by curiosity, that righteous-souled one spoke, unfolding the true cause of that noise.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-24-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1023006","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskr